<td>Purchase</td>
<td>1,400.00 [qid:de117efd]</td>
<td>Amortise per asset, then reuse each asset's existing Usage-service team weights (the same weights that already produce the direct split for VMware Licensing, Colo Power &amp; Cooling and Storage Array)</td>
<td>Blocked: no per-asset breakdown of Purchase in the figures</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Tax</td>
<td>111.78 [qid:de117efd]</td>
<td>Follow-the-base, pro rata to whatever line it is levied on, so it inherits that line's team split</td>
<td>Blocked: the figures do not state the tax base</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Both rules are mechanically safe, in that they add no new tagging requirement and reuse weights that already exist. Neither can be applied from the data in hand.</p>
<p>Note for whoever runs this: Tax is 111.78 in 2026-07 and 0.00 in 2026-08 [qid:5a353afd]. A rule tuned on the July shape will allocate nothing in August. That is a data-shape question for a human, not an allocation defect.</p>
<h2>3. Post-application AAI</h2>
<p>Not measurable from these figures, and I will not estimate it. The deliverable the card asks for requires the allocation engine to be re-run on 2026-07 with the two rules above in place, which produces a new AAI figure with its own qid. What I can state:</p>
<ul>
<li>The only lever available on AAI is the Purchase and Tax pool [qid:de117efd]. Nothing in the shared-asset split rules touches it.</li>
<li>Direct allocation is already at its ceiling: 2,477.13 allocated against 2,477.13 of Usage [qid:f8b13338].</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Ask:</strong> a per-asset breakdown of the 1,400.00 Purchase pool and the tax base, then a re-measured <code>aai_pct</code> with a fresh qid. Until then any post-application AAI number would be invented.</p>
<h2>4. Task #68: storage growth after the array step</h2>
<p>Storage Array runs 455.42 in 2026-07 against 223.93 in 2026-08 [qid:9f2f688b]. August is partial, through 2026-08-15 [qid:0925bda9], so it covers roughly half the month, and the partial figure sits at roughly half the prior full month. That is the shape of a flat run rate. <strong>No growth step is visible after the array step.</strong></p>
<p>The same flat pattern holds across the whole desk: every top service and every team shows a roughly halved delta on the half-month window [qid:9f2f688b], and the forecast winner is a moving average rather than anything trend-bearing [qid:cbaa95ba]. Storage is not diverging from the desk.</p>
<p>Caveat, stated rather than papered over: the figures contain no same-length window comparison and no capacity or headroom series, so this is a level check, not a capacity check. If #68 was meant to establish headroom, that needs a capacity figure the desk does not currently compute.</p>
<h2>5. Request #5, storage array tranche 2 (610 USD)</h2>
<p>The 610 USD carries no qid, so it is outside the ground truth and I cannot cite, validate or endorse it. Setting that aside, the evidence this request rests on does not support it:</p>
<ul>
<li>No storage growth step is present after the first array step (section 4).</li>
<li>No headroom, utilisation or capacity figure exists in the data to establish a need.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Recommendation: hold.</strong> Route to a human with two specific asks, a utilisation or headroom measurement on the array, and a qid'd cost figure for the tranche. Approving on a flat storage series and an uncited number would be a purchase without a measured trigger.</p>
<h2>6. Anomalies: explained by shape, not worth money</h2>
<p>Ranked by money, as the house rule requires, there is nothing here. The full anomaly excess across 2026-07 and 2026-08 is 5.81 USD against 3,694.22 USD of usage, 0.16%, green band [qid:29a3c255]. The largest single item is 2.62 USD of excess on VMware Licensing, 2026-01-05 [qid:6666867a].</p>
<p>Ranked by z-score they look alarming (up to 10.47), which is exactly the inversion the house rule exists to prevent. Two observations for the detector owner rather than the budget owner:</p>
<ul>
<li>Four of the five items are VMware Licensing on <code>sre-platform</code> inside one week of January 2026 [qid:6666867a]. One service, one team, one week is a pattern, and it suggests a baseline that has not learned a recurring January licensing event.</li>
<li><strong>None of these is explained by a known driver.</strong> Both registered drivers are cloud-scoped (the 2026-08-02 heraldyx launch on EC2, the 2026-08-04 cloud range exercise). Neither covers onprem in January. The <code>driver</code> field is null on all five items.</li>
</ul>
<p>So: needs a human look, but a detector-tuning look, not a spend look. At 0.16% of spend this consumes no budget attention.</p>
<h2>7. Forward level</h2>
<p>The 30-day usage forecast is 2,417.26 USD [qid:2c1e49a2], band 2,363.86 to 2,470.66, from moving-average(28), the lowest-MAPE model of the three tested at 1.4728 [qid:cbaa95ba]. Two things a reader should not miss: this forecasts Usage only, so the flat 1,400.00 Purchase pool sits entirely outside it, and it is a flat-level model, which is consistent with the no-growth reading in section 4 but also means it would not anticipate a step change if tranche 2 were approved.</p>
<h2>Figures I needed and do not have</h2>
<ol>
<li>Per-asset breakdown of the 1,400.00 Purchase pool (gates both split rules and the AAI re-measure).</li>
<li>The tax base for the 111.78 Tax line.</li>
<li>Re-measured <code>aai_pct</code> after rule application, with a qid.</li>
<li>Array capacity or utilisation headroom (gates request #5).</li>
<li>A qid'd cost figure for storage array tranche 2; the 610 USD in the request is uncited.</li>
</ol>
